# What acb records and what agents may see. Each line is `<level> <pattern>`, with the patterns
# of .gitignore: *, **, ?, [abc], a trailing / for folders, a leading / to anchor to this folder.
# The last matching line wins. Any folder can have its own .acbrules for the paths below it.
#
# Built-in levels:
#   track   recorded and shared, agents read it (every file that no rule names)
#   ignore  not recorded at all
#   local   recorded on this machine only, never exported or synced
#   secret  shared with its values sealed; agents see the keys and layout, not the values
#   vault   shared and sealed whole; agents see nothing
# Sealed values are encrypted whenever they leave this machine; `acb key` shows who can open them.
# This root file can add or redefine levels: `level <name> = <words>`, where the words are
# shared, local or untracked; readable, masked or hidden; and `sealed`. For example:
#   level config = shared readable sealed
# ACB_READ=secret,vault (or *) lets a reader, such as a trusted agent, see those levels in full.

ignore .DS_Store
ignore node_modules/
ignore target/
ignore dist/
ignore build/
ignore __pycache__/
ignore .venv/
ignore *.pyc
ignore *.log

secret .env
secret .env.*
secret *.env
track .env.example
vault *.pem
vault *.key
vault id_rsa*
